 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1033 1875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050642 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5ba25c95-b937-5921-95e0-7207aa7b83f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1031 "The Domain of a Transformed Radical Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1033 1600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050850 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6bcb4fdf-14db-5859-bc2c-c769917007f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1031 "The Domain of a Multivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1033 1899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1034 1.1523219435529348 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "77b779f9-f0f6-53e5-821f-44c6d9f4ce47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124357 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124357 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the domain of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5}-2$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a085da3-d8eb-52eb-94ea-4969fb74a83f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124357 #edb/ref 1074 "The domain of\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5} -2\n$$\n\nwe replace $x$ with $3x+5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n{3x+5} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x + 5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge - 5 \\\\ x & \\ge - \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5}{3}, \\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f94991ce01ad98ec5bdffbb0673182dc40bc264803b6eea5154241985359c0b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124358 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124358 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values are possible outputs of $f(x) = 2 \\sqrt{x- 2} + 2$?\n\n1. $1$\n2. $2$\n3. $6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124358 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf9e3a92-47fa-5969-8d05-2d28114f3036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124358 #edb/ref 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-2} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x - 2} + 2 & \\ge 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in [2,\\infty)$.\n\nAmong the given values, only\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x)=6\n$$\n\nlie in the range. So, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10d9a611e2fbf8ebf6ae3c12b57e51ea100bf13663586379c42293d4ee45d183.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124359 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124359 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c$. Now consider the following properties of $f(x)$:\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$.\n- The range is $f(x) \\in [2, \\infty)$.\n- The point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124359 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdb0a166-a880-5efb-a747-ac8f940ff50b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124359 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}b - x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{bmatrix}$\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right]$, which is equivalent to $x\\le 2$. Hence, we obtain\n$b=2$.\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0$. Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2$, we must have\n$a\\sqrt{2 - x}_(⏟)_(\\ge 0) + c \\ge 2$.\nThis means that $c=2$.\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have\n$f(1)|= a\\sqrt{2 - 1} + 2; 4|= a + 2; a|= 2$.\n\nTherefore, our function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124389 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124389 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt{6x+7} - 5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa7bd088-d496-5862-80a0-cd20083b2754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124389 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6x+7} - 5 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{6x + 7} - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{6x + 7} &= 5 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{6x + 7})^{2} &= 5^{2} \\\\\n6x + 7 &= 25 \\\\\n6x &= 18 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{6 \\cdot 3 + 7} - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{25} &= 5 \\\\\n5 &= 5✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124548 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124549 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124550 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124550 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124550 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124550 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124550 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124581 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124581 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124581 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124581 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124581 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Transformed Square Root Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124687 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "15348d0c-df15-5fed-9671-f77adb0de3d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125449 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125449 #edb/ref 1061 21142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125449 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12034629-2e9d-5e9e-9428-382566508681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125449 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125450 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125450 #edb/ref 1061 21143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125450 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00a42fa0-7cac-5722-a8c6-a9c77cbe1379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125450 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125451 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125451 #edb/ref 1061 21144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125451 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddc439de-a04d-570e-a224-ae11bc6f93b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125451 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125452 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125452 #edb/ref 1061 21145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125452 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3976c517-061c-59fb-bf37-f3bd18026474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125452 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125453 #edb/ref 1061 21146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125453 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5da1008-3709-56f8-9b9d-9e24db297a01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125453 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125507 #edb/ref 1061 29636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90d4f739-6ff8-5669-b57b-b2a88263ccc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125511 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125511 #edb/ref 1061 29854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125511 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df14bf6d-23b8-5ed1-9e7b-0662c3ed3d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125511 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125802 #edb/ref 1064 "The domain of a radical function is limited to the set of $x$-values that make the expression under the square root positive or zero.\n\nFor example, the domain of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nor $x \\in [0,\\infty)$ in interval notation.\n\nOn the other hand, the domain of $\\sqrt{-x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n-x \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx \\leq 0\n$$\n\nThus, the domain of $\\sqrt{-x}$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 0]$.\n\nAs another example, the domain of $\\sqrt{x-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nx -1 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nor $x \\in [1, \\infty)$ in interval notation.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-1}\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cbae5f645d7774e114c483df27c8493c7e6a7f4fcf807ac7a14c54e4afa62105.png)\n\nIn general, to find the domain of a radical function of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{g(x)}\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\ng(x) \\geq 0\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125802 #edb/ref 1065 3797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125802 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "149ca930-55b2-5261-97a0-91c331aecf92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125802 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125803 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's examine the range of a transformed radical function. For example, what is the range of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt{5x-2}+7\n$$\n\nThe range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x-2} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then adding $7$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5x - 2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5x - 2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5x - 2} + 7 & \\le 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,7]$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/2651dbfeff426df9973c56c60e1d947a069b5b8c7a12281b095a878ba3de0f42.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125803 #edb/ref 1065 3798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125803 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "709c3ae9-1f28-596c-acf1-bd755cb5f318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125803 #edb/ref 1067 "The Range of a Transformed Square Root Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125827 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the roots of a transformed radical function, we must set the function expression equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFor instance, what are the roots of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt{2x+1} + 5\n$$\n\nIn this case, we set\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x+1} + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 1} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 1} &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125827 #edb/ref 1065 4810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125827 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a0803789-82d9-5ffd-91db-c1d02974a064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125827 #edb/ref 1067 "The Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
